## `for mut x in list` (own.excl.5). A struct element is a pointer to its
## fields, so a write through `x` changes the list's element in place. Any
## other element is written back by `assign_param` (lower_param.ail) on each
## assignment to `x`, so `break`, `return` and `continue` need no write-back.
use ast

## The loop variable `var` of a `for mut` over `list`, at index `i`. A struct
## element is in place already; a tuple, list, map or set is stored into the
## element's object (as for a `mut` parameter), and any other type is stored
## back into the list on each assignment to the variable.
fn Lower.note_mut_elem(mut self, var str, list int, i int, e Ty)
    key := lookup(var) else return
    if needs_copy(e) or e.is_coll()
        facts.mut_ptr["{fname}|{key}"] = read_var(key, cur)
    else
        facts.mut_elem["{fname}|{key}"] = [list, i]
